===== what the outside reader found in the produced bytes (docs/research/pdfa-switch-readers.py)
  both paths   : identification `pdfaid:part`/`conformance` present and equal to the part the card names
                 (1/B, 2/B and 3/B were each driven), XMP parses as RDF, is strict UTF-8 with the BOM in the
                 xpacket prolog, trailer /ID present, /MarkInfo /Marked true.
  output intent: /S /GTS_PDFA1 with /DestOutputProfile → a 476-byte ICC profile whose header is
                 self-consistent and which lcms2 opens and names 'sRGB IEC61966-2.1 (BrowserSnip
                 minimal)' in BOTH paths:
                 declared length 476 = actual, `acsp` at 36, device class `mntr`, colour space `RGB `, PCS `XYZ `,
                 9 tags, tag table ending exactly at byte 476; the stream dictionary carries /N 3 and the metadata
                 stream /N 0 (ISO 32000-1 Table 162). The engine writes /OutputConditionIdentifier, /RegistryName
                 and /Info as **strings**; the local path writes the same three as **names** (`/sRGB#20IEC61966-2.1`).
  fonts        : the engine output leaves MuPDF naming 2 faces whose subset carries a font program
                 (veraPDF's embedding rule passes on it, 0 failed); the local path ships the same 27
                 standard-14 references it inherited and veraPDF fails exactly that rule — which is the
                 one failure the card already names in words ("1 of 1 font(s) are not embedded …
                 a validator will reject this file as it stands").

===== reading (this chunk's verdict)
* Engine path (Ghostscript fetched): **verified good by veraPDF** — colour.pdf at 2b `isCompliant=true, 144 rules
  passed, 0 failed, 1105 checks`, meta.pdf at 1b `isCompliant=true, 129/0`. It also keeps what the document has:
  annotations 3→3, outline 3→3, text characters equal, page count equal. Two measured losses: the file's single
  /Link annotation is gone (annotation objects 4→3, MuPDF links 1→0) and page ink moves (+3 779 px at 36 dpi on
  annot.pdf) because the faces are subset and re-embedded. Neither is said on the card, which claims only the
  structures it writes.
* Local path (engine unreachable), the honest-degraded branch: the sentence names itself
  (`Wrote PDF/A-… identification … with this app's own path, not Ghostscript`), names the fonts it cannot embed,
  and adds `no validator ran, so conformance to ISO 19005 is not claimed` — so it never *claims* a level. But
  three duties the same path writes are measurably wrong, and two are new to this chunk:
    1. **§6.1.4 test 3 — “Xref streams shall not be used”.** PDF/A-1 forbids the cross-reference stream, and the
       local save writes one (`/Type /XRef` present in every local output; absent in every engine output). So
       choosing `PDF/A-1b` on the local path cannot produce a part-1 file at all, whatever else is right.
    2. **§6.7.3 tests 4 and 5 — Subject and Keywords are not mirrored.** `/Info` of meta.pdf carries
       `Subject (A subject worth removing)` and `Keywords (alpha, beta, gamma)`; the packet holds only
       dc:title, dc:creator, xmp:CreateDate/ModifyDate/MetadataDate and xmp:CreatorTool — no `dc:description`,
       no `pdf:Keywords`. The last round's fix claimed every analogue was mirrored; four are not.
    3. **§6.7.3 tests 1 and 8 — the dates are not equivalent**: the packet writes
       `2026-11-25T11:01:39.376Z` against a `/Info` `D:20261125110139Z`, and PDF dates carry no milliseconds.
  With all four aside, the only failure left on the local path at 2b is the un-embedded fonts the card already
  names — `143 rules passed, 1 failed` — which is the honest floor of a tab without Ghostscript.
* Transparency: 0 soft masks and 0 alpha graphic states on the driven fixtures, so the part-1 transparency refusal
  path was not reachable here; recorded rather than tested.
* Network half: 9/9 runs, `file bytes posted: none`; when not blocked the only off-origin traffic is the documented
  engine fetch (`@bentopdf/gs-wasm@0.1.1/assets/`), plus Google Fonts for the shell.

